course: dictionary
lesson: 3249
title: The flap at the back of your throat
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Your throat is a busy place. Air goes down it to your lungs, and food and drink go down it to your stomach. The epiglottis is a small flap that helps keep these two journeys apart.
explain: When you swallow, the epiglottis closes, so food goes down to your stomach and not into your lungs. When you breathe, it opens again so air can reach your windpipe.
explain: Read the context to work out what the word means. «When you swallow, a flap called the epiglottis covers the windpipe.» The clue is in the word flap: it is a thin piece that opens and shuts, like a little door.
explain: Now read the rest of the sentence: «If you laugh or talk while swallowing, some drink can slip into the windpipe and you cough it out.» This tells you what happens when the flap is late. Your body coughs to push the drink back out.
fact: The epiglottis is a small flap at the back of your throat.
fact: The epiglottis closes when you swallow, so food goes down to your stomach.
fact: The epiglottis opens again when you breathe, so air can reach your windpipe.
fact: If food or drink goes «down the wrong way», the epiglottis may not have closed in time, and you cough.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence: «The epiglottis covers the windpipe» makes sense if the epiglottis is a flap.
exercise: What does the epiglottis do when you swallow? | It makes your voice louder | *It closes so food goes down to your stomach | It pumps blood round your body | It stays wide open so air can get in
exercise: In «a flap called the epiglottis covers the windpipe», what kind of thing is the epiglottis? | A pipe that carries air | A type of cough | A kind of drink | *A flap
exercise: «If you laugh or talk while swallowing, some drink can slip into the windpipe.» What usually makes you cough? | Breathing in slowly through your nose | Sitting down after a meal | *Drink going into the windpipe | Closing your eyes
test: Which phrase from the lessons means the flap shuts? | *the epiglottis closes | flap of the epiglottis | windpipe and lungs | air reaches the windpipe
test: Why does the epiglottis open again? | *So air can reach your windpipe when you breathe | To make your voice louder | To stop you blinking | To let food into your lungs
exam: When food goes «down the wrong way», what has most likely happened? | *The epiglottis did not close in time | Your windpipe has grown bigger | You have swallowed a flap by mistake | Your stomach has closed up
